{
  "module": "Attention: how tokens exchange information",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "attention-output",
    "title": "The attention output, self and cross",
    "summary": "How the attention weights are used: each output is a weighted average of the value vectors. Also, self-attention versus cross-attention.",
    "covers": ["attention-output", "self-vs-cross-attention"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "scores-weights",
        "reason": "The output uses the attention weights."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "qkv-projections",
        "reason": "The values are the V vectors."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "weighted-average",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The weights say how much each position matters. The "
            },
            {
              "text": "values",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " say what each position has to give. The output for position i combines them:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "o_i = \\sum_j A_{ij}\\, v_j",
          "spoken": "o i equals the sum over j of A i j times v j"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "This is a "
            },
            {
              "text": "weighted average of the value vectors",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", with weights that are non-negative and add up to 1."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 33)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "toy-output",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The toy case: values v₁ = (1, 0), v₂ = (0, 1), v₃ = (2, 2). Query 1 has weights 0.422, 0.155, 0.422."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Its output is 0.422 × (1, 0) + 0.155 × (0, 1) + 0.422 × (2, 2) = (1.267, 1.000)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Because the weights are a weighted average, the result stays inside the range of the values it mixes."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 33)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "shapes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "As matrices: A is n × n and V is n × d_v, so A · V is n × d_v. Each of the n positions gets its own new vector of length d_v."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "\\mathrm{Attention}(Q,K,V) = \\mathrm{softmax}\\!\\left(\\frac{Q K^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) V",
          "spoken": "Attention of Q, K, V equals softmax of Q K transposed over the square root of d k, times V"
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 31)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "the-picture",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Here is the whole path for one head, from X to the head output."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "src": "/schematics/transformer-attention-qkv.svg",
          "alt": "Scaled dot-product attention for one head: X is projected by W_Q, W_K and W_V to Q, K and V. The scores Q times K transpose over root d_k are passed through a causal mask and a row-wise softmax, then multiplied by V to give the head output.",
          "display": "expandable"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Ignore the causal mask step and the note about several heads for now. Both get their own lessons."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 31)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "self-attention",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "In everything so far, Q, K and V were all made from the "
            },
            {
              "text": "same",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " sequence X. That is "
            },
            {
              "text": "self-attention",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": the sequence attends to itself."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 36)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "cross-attention",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "There is a second arrangement, "
            },
            {
              "text": "cross-attention",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". The "
            },
            {
              "text": "queries",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " come from one sequence, and the "
            },
            {
              "text": "keys and values",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " come from another."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It is used in models that read one sequence and write another, such as the original Transformer and T5. The formulas are unchanged. Only the source of Q and of K and V differs."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 36)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "What is the attention output for one position?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "correct": true,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "A weighted average of the value vectors, using that position's attention weights"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "o_i = the sum over j of A_ij times v_j."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The value vector with the largest weight, on its own"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "All the values contribute, in proportion to their weights."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The average of the query vectors"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The values are averaged, not the queries."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The sum of the scores in its row"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The scores are turned into weights, and the weights mix the values."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "teaches": ["weighted-average"],
